<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Nupe Kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">1531 CE – 1835 CE (approx.) · capital Gbara</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Kingdom of the Niger valley above the confluence, founded by the hero Tsoede and famed for its glass, brass and cloth.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Nupe tradition credits <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsoede">Tsoede</a>, also called Edegi, a son of the Attah of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/igala-kingdom">Igala Kingdom</a> by a Nupe mother, with uniting the Nupe chiefdoms in the early 16th century. Travelling up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> in a bronze canoe, he founded the capital at Gbara and gave the kingdom its royal regalia.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etsu-nupe">Etsu Nupe</a> ruled over farmers, fishermen and skilled craftsmen whose glass <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a>, brassware and cloth were traded widely. Nupe controlled the river between <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/hausa-kingdoms">Hausaland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> country and fought the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/oyo-empire">Oyo Empire</a>, sometimes paying it tribute and sometimes defeating it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> spread among the elite from the 18th century.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the early 19th century rival claimants Majiya and Jimada fought for the throne, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> cleric <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mallam-dendo">Mallam Dendo</a> exploited the conflict. His descendants took power, and Nupe was absorbed into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a>, later as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bida-emirate">Bida Emirate</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etsu-nupe">Etsu Nupe</a> still reigns at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bida">Bida</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/nupe-kingdom.jpg" alt="Nupe Kingdom" width="320" height="306"><figcaption>Image: JelaniZ · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Place_of_Etsue_Nupe.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1531 CE – 1835 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Gbara</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Tsoede, Majiya II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Islam and Nupe traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Nupe</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/nigeria">Nigeria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Nupe, Kingdom of Nupe</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1683&amp;focus=nupe-kingdom" aria-label="Nupe Kingdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4846 1838 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Nupe Kingdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5166 2094L5186 2169M5166 2094L5166 2094"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5186 2169h0M5166 2094h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5166 2094h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5166 2094h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1683&amp;focus=nupe-kingdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
